.TH USERMOD 1 "September 2026" "mitos-utils 0.1.0" "MITOS User Commands"
.SH NAME
usermod \- modify an existing user account
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B usermod
[\fB\-c\fR \fIcomment\fR] [\fB\-d\fR \fIhome\fR [\fB\-m\fR]] [\fB\-g\fR \fIgroup\fR] [\fB\-aG\fR|\fB\-G\fR \fIgroups\fR] [\fB\-s\fR \fIshell\fR] [\fB\-u\fR \fIuid\fR] [\fB\-L\fR|\fB\-U\fR] \fIusername\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
Modify \fIusername\fR's entry in \fB/etc/passwd\fR, \fB/etc/shadow\fR, and \fB/etc/group\fR. Must be run as root (or installed setuid-root) -- unlike \fBpasswd\fR(1), there is no self-service mode.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-c \fIcomment\fR
Set the GECOS/comment field.
.TP
.B \-d \fIhome\fR
Set the home directory field. Combine with \fB\-m\fR to move the existing home directory's contents to the new location (same-filesystem rename only, see LIMITATIONS).
.TP
.B \-g \fIgroup\fR
Set the primary group by name; the group must already exist.
.TP
.B \-aG \fIgroups\fR
Append \fIusername\fR to each comma-separated supplementary group, without removing it from any group it's already in.
.TP
.B \-G \fIgroups\fR
Replace the full supplementary-group list with exactly the given comma-separated groups, removing \fIusername\fR from any group not named -- matches real \fBusermod\fR(8)'s own semantics. The summary line printed on success always states exactly what was added and removed.
.TP
.B \-u \fIuid\fR
Set the numeric uid. Refuses a uid already in use by another account.
.TP
.B \-L
Lock the account -- the same \fB!\fR-prefix convention \fBpasswd\fR(1) and a freshly \fBuseradd\fR(1)'d account both use.
.TP
.B \-U
Unlock the account. Refuses if it was never given a real password in the first place, since that would reveal an empty hash -- run \fBpasswd\fR(1) first in that case.
.SH DELIBERATELY NARROWER THAN REAL USERMOD
No \fB\-l\fR (rename -- would have to ripple through \fB/etc/shadow\fR's key, every group's member list, and conventionally the home directory path too, so it's left out entirely rather than half-done), no \fB\-o\fR (permit a duplicate uid). Changing \fB\-u\fR or \fB\-g\fR does not re-\fBchown\fR files scattered elsewhere on the filesystem -- only a home directory just moved by \fB\-d \-m\fR in the same invocation gets its ownership fixed up -- matching real \fBusermod\fR(8)'s own default.
.SH LIMITATIONS
\fB\-d \-m\fR only performs a same-filesystem rename; a cross-filesystem home directory move is refused with an error explaining how to move it by hand first, rather than attempting an unverified recursive copy.
.SH SECURITY
.B This has not been independently security-reviewed.
Modifies \fB/etc/passwd\fR, \fB/etc/shadow\fR, and \fB/etc/group\fR directly, under the same lock file real shadow-utils uses. Read \fBcommon::accounts\fR in the source before installing this setuid anywhere that matters -- the same weight as \fBsu\fR(1)/\fBsudo\fR(1)/\fBuseradd\fR(1)/\fBgroupadd\fR(1)/\fBpasswd\fR(1)'s own SECURITY sections. Get it reviewed and test it in a disposable VM first.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
Every mitos-utils command additionally accepts \fB\-\-help\fR and \fB\-\-version\fR, and \fB\-\-\fR to mark the end of options.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, non-zero if the account (or a named group) doesn't exist, the caller isn't root, no changes were requested, or the database couldn't be locked/written.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR useradd (1),
.BR passwd (1),
.BR groupadd (1),
.BR mitos\-box (1)
